  "name": "desert-mesa-block",
  "notes": "Bedded sandstone worked by wind from one quarter. Two mechanisms, and both are the desert: the flat top and the stepped flanks are bed surfaces, not a cut plane, and aspect scours the windward side harder than the lee, which is the mark that says desert rather than merely dry. Sits shallow because there is no soil to sink into. Bedding was tried harder than this - 0.75 with erode 0.60 - and rejected: the beds are only as visible as the rock that has gone, so turning them up means turning erosion up, and at that setting the block reads as shale rather than sandstone and loses the flat top that is half of what makes it a mesa block.",
